### What is the Factory Method pattern?

Factory Method centralises the decision of which concrete class to create behind a method. The caller asks for a product by a key and never writes `new`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface SettlementStrategy {
    void settle(String tradeId, BigDecimal amount);
}

public class EquitySettlement implements SettlementStrategy {
    public void settle(String tradeId, BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Settling equity trade " + tradeId + ": " + amount);
    }
}

public class FxSettlement implements SettlementStrategy {
    public void settle(String tradeId, BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Settling FX trade " + tradeId + ": " + amount);
    }
}

public class SettlementFactory {
    public static SettlementStrategy create(String tradeType) {
        return switch (tradeType) {
            case "EQUITY" -> new EquitySettlement();
            case "FX" -> new FxSettlement();
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown: " + tradeType);
        };
    }
}
```

Use it when you have one product hierarchy and the choice depends on input or configuration.

### What is the Abstract Factory pattern?

Abstract Factory produces families of related products that must be used together. Each concrete factory produces one complete, consistent family.

```java
package com.bank.report;

public interface ReportHeader {
    String header();
}

public interface ReportFormatter {
    String format(String content);
}

// Abstract Factory interface
public interface ReportKitFactory {
    ReportHeader createHeader();
    ReportFormatter createFormatter();
}

// Regulatory family
public class RegulatoryHeader implements ReportHeader {
    public String header() { return "[REGULATORY FILING]"; }
}
public class RegulatoryFormatter implements ReportFormatter {
    public String format(String c) { return "FORMAL: " + c; }
}
public class RegulatoryKitFactory implements ReportKitFactory {
    public ReportHeader createHeader() { return new RegulatoryHeader(); }
    public ReportFormatter createFormatter() { return new RegulatoryFormatter(); }
}

// Customer family
public class CustomerHeader implements ReportHeader {
    public String header() { return "Dear Valued Customer,"; }
}
public class CustomerFormatter implements ReportFormatter {
    public String format(String c) { return "Friendly: " + c; }
}
public class CustomerKitFactory implements ReportKitFactory {
    public ReportHeader createHeader() { return new CustomerHeader(); }
    public ReportFormatter createFormatter() { return new CustomerFormatter(); }
}
```

Swapping the factory swaps the entire matching set.

### What is the Builder pattern?

Builder solves the telescoping-constructor problem. Each field gets a named setter that returns the builder itself, and `build()` validates and produces an immutable object.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public final class TradeOrder {
    private final String tradeId;
    private final String symbol;
    private final int quantity;
    private final BigDecimal price;
    private final BigDecimal fee;

    private TradeOrder(Builder b) {
        this.tradeId = b.tradeId;
        this.symbol = b.symbol;
        this.quantity = b.quantity;
        this.price = b.price;
        this.fee = b.fee;
    }

    public static Builder builder() { return new Builder(); }

    public static class Builder {
        private String tradeId;
        private String symbol;
        private int quantity;
        private BigDecimal price;
        private BigDecimal fee = BigDecimal.ZERO;

        public Builder tradeId(String v) { this.tradeId = v; return this; }
        public Builder symbol(String v) { this.symbol = v; return this; }
        public Builder quantity(int v) { this.quantity = v; return this; }
        public Builder price(BigDecimal v) { this.price = v; return this; }
        public Builder fee(BigDecimal v) { this.fee = v; return this; }

        public TradeOrder build() {
            if (tradeId == null || symbol == null) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("tradeId and symbol are required");
            }
            if (quantity <= 0) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("quantity must be positive");
            }
            return new TradeOrder(this);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return tradeId + ": " + quantity + " " + symbol + " @ " + price + " fee=" + fee;
    }
}
```

### What is the Prototype pattern?

Prototype creates a new object by copying an existing one. Use a copy constructor rather than `Cloneable`.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class OrderTemplate {
    private final String exchange;
    private final BigDecimal defaultFee;
    private final List<String> tags;

    public OrderTemplate(String exchange, BigDecimal defaultFee, List<String> tags) {
        this.exchange = exchange;
        this.defaultFee = defaultFee;
        this.tags = new ArrayList<>(tags);
    }

    // Copy constructor – deep copy
    public OrderTemplate(OrderTemplate other) {
        this.exchange = other.exchange;
        this.defaultFee = other.defaultFee;
        this.tags = new ArrayList<>(other.tags); // deep copy the mutable list
    }

    public OrderTemplate copy() {
        return new OrderTemplate(this);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return exchange + " fee=" + defaultFee + " tags=" + tags;
    }
}
```

### When do you use each pattern?

| Pattern | Question it answers | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| Factory Method | Which one concrete type? | One product hierarchy, choice depends on input |
| Abstract Factory | Which matching family? | Multiple related products must be consistent |
| Builder | How to assemble step by step? | Many optional fields, immutable result |
| Prototype | How to copy an existing object? | Expensive construction, pre-configured templates |

### How do these patterns look in modern Java?

Factory Method often becomes a `Map<String, Supplier<Product>>`, eliminating the switch entirely:

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

public class ModernSettlementFactory {
    private static final Map<String, Supplier<SettlementStrategy>> REGISTRY = Map.of(
        "EQUITY", EquitySettlement::new,
        "FX", FxSettlement::new
    );

    public static SettlementStrategy create(String type) {
        Supplier<SettlementStrategy> supplier = REGISTRY.get(type);
        if (supplier == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown: " + type);
        return supplier.get();
    }
}
```

Prototype uses a copy constructor instead of `Cloneable`. Builder is often generated by Lombok's `@Builder`.

### What are common mistakes with creational patterns?

The most common mistake is using a Singleton where a plain injected dependency would do, adding global state and complicating testing. Another error is choosing Factory Method when a simple constructor is sufficient, adding a layer of indirection for no benefit. A third mistake is using Builder for objects with only two or three required fields, where a constructor is clearer.

```java
package com.bank.pattern;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CreationalMistakes {
    // WRONG: Singleton for a stateless service
    // public class TransferValidator {
    //     private static final TransferValidator INSTANCE = new TransferValidator();
    //     public static TransferValidator getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }
    // }

    // CORRECT: Let Spring inject it as a bean
    // @Service public class TransferValidator { ... }

    // WRONG: Builder for a simple 2-field object
    // Transaction t = Transaction.builder().amount(x).type(y).build();

    // CORRECT: Just use the constructor
    // Transaction t = new Transaction(amount, type);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: Singleton for stateless service (use DI instead)");
        System.out.println("WRONG: Factory for one concrete type (use constructor)");
        System.out.println("WRONG: Builder for 2-field object (overkill)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: Use creational patterns when the problem matches:");
        System.out.println("  Factory: multiple variants selected at runtime");
        System.out.println("  Builder: many optional parameters");
        System.out.println("  Prototype: expensive-to-create objects with small variation");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: Singleton for stateless service (use DI instead)
WRONG: Factory for one concrete type (use constructor)
WRONG: Builder for 2-field object (overkill)

CORRECT: Use creational patterns when the problem matches:
  Factory: multiple variants selected at runtime
  Builder: many optional parameters
  Prototype: expensive-to-create objects with small variation
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Factory Method and Abstract Factory answer which type to create. Builder and Prototype answer how to assemble or copy.
- In Spring, the bean container itself is a Factory. Injecting a `Map<String, Interface>` gives a factory for free.
- Overusing Abstract Factory adds interfaces for no benefit if products do not need to match.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class CreationalPatternsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Factory
        SettlementStrategy s = SettlementFactory.create("EQUITY");
        s.settle("TRD-001", new BigDecimal("500000"));

        // Builder
        TradeOrder order = TradeOrder.builder()
            .tradeId("TRD-002").symbol("RELIANCE")
            .quantity(100).price(new BigDecimal("2450"))
            .fee(new BigDecimal("50")).build();
        System.out.println(order);

        // Prototype
        OrderTemplate template = new OrderTemplate("NSE", new BigDecimal("20"), List.of("DEFAULT"));
        OrderTemplate copy = template.copy();
        System.out.println("Template: " + template);
        System.out.println("Copy: " + copy);
    }
}
```

```text
Settling equity trade TRD-001: 500000
TRD-002: 100 RELIANCE @ 2450 fee=50
Template: NSE fee=20 tags=[DEFAULT]
Copy: NSE fee=20 tags=[DEFAULT]
```
